1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,155
Today, an AI can draft a
product spec in seconds.

2
00:00:05,805 --> 00:00:10,565
It can summarize a thousand customer
interviews overnight, write the code, and

3
00:00:10,585 --> 00:00:12,106
build the dashboard.

4
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:15,884
So what is left for the product manager?

5
00:00:30,734 --> 00:00:33,311
To answer that, look at three horizons.

6
00:00:33,861 --> 00:00:36,050
The first is
already here: AI.

7
00:00:36,070 --> 00:00:40,454
Tools that write, analyze,
design, and code alongside us.

8
00:00:41,004 --> 00:00:45,390
The second may be coming: AGI,
artificial general intelligence.

9
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:49,928
Systems that could match people
at most kinds of mental work.

10
00:00:50,478 --> 00:00:55,337
The third is still speculative:
ASI, artificial superintelligence.

11
00:00:55,357 --> 00:00:59,467
Systems that would surpass
us in nearly every field.

12
00:01:00,217 --> 00:01:01,981
No one knows the timeline.

13
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:07,531
Some expect these horizons within years,
others within decades, and some doubt they

14
00:01:07,551 --> 00:01:08,991
will come at all.

15
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:12,260
So the smart move isn't
to predict the future.

16
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,336
It's to prepare for all of it.

17
00:01:16,886 --> 00:01:19,090
Start with what's changing right now.

18
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:23,763
Much of a product manager's week
has always been production work:

19
00:01:23,783 --> 00:01:29,027
writing documents, synthesizing research,
analyzing data, drafting tickets, and

20
00:01:29,047 --> 00:01:30,481
reporting status.

21
00:01:30,931 --> 00:01:33,318
AI is absorbing that work, fast.

22
00:01:33,968 --> 00:01:38,835
A single product manager can now build a
working prototype in an afternoon, put it

23
00:01:38,855 --> 00:01:43,580
in front of real users that evening,
and read the results the next morning.

24
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:46,207
The cost of building is collapsing.

25
00:01:46,227 --> 00:01:50,205
And when building gets cheap,
something else becomes expensive.

26
00:01:51,955 --> 00:01:53,296
Human judgment.

27
00:01:54,346 --> 00:01:57,093
For decades, the hard
part was building things.

28
00:01:57,113 --> 00:02:01,917
Now the hard part is knowing what to
build, and knowing whether it's any good.

29
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:06,813
When almost anything can be built, the
scarcest skill is deciding what's worth

30
00:02:06,833 --> 00:02:07,633
building.

31
00:02:10,373 --> 00:02:12,495
Now picture the second horizon.

32
00:02:12,845 --> 00:02:15,653
AI agents that don't just assist, but act.

33
00:02:15,673 --> 00:02:20,717
They research the market, design the
interface, write and test the code, and

34
00:02:20,737 --> 00:02:22,302
analyze the launch.

35
00:02:22,852 --> 00:02:27,523
In that world, the product manager
becomes an orchestrator of intelligence:

36
00:02:27,543 --> 00:02:32,584
setting the goal, the constraints, and
the definition of success, then steering a

37
00:02:32,604 --> 00:02:35,137
team of people and agents toward it.

38
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:39,182
You won't manage a backlog.
You'll manage outcomes.

39
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:43,295
And the third horizon?

40
00:02:43,845 --> 00:02:48,403
If machines ever surpass us at nearly
everything, the question shifts from how

41
00:02:48,423 --> 00:02:50,934
to build, to what should exist at all.

42
00:02:51,584 --> 00:02:55,755
Someone still has to decide what
matters. Whose problems come first.

43
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:57,760
Which trade-offs are acceptable.

44
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,068
And who is accountable
when something goes wrong.

45
00:03:01,718 --> 00:03:05,212
Those are questions of values,
trust, and responsibility.

46
00:03:05,232 --> 00:03:07,118
And they belong to people.

47
00:03:12,468 --> 00:03:16,823
So, is product management a good
career for someone starting today?

48
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:20,283
Yes. But not the old
version of the job.

49
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:25,924
The product manager who mostly writes
documents, runs meetings, and reports

50
00:03:25,944 --> 00:03:27,475
status is at real risk.

51
00:03:27,495 --> 00:03:32,902
Much of that work is being automated, and
entry-level roles built on it are getting

52
00:03:32,922 --> 00:03:34,206
harder to find.

53
00:03:34,856 --> 00:03:39,803
But the product manager who understands
customers deeply, builds with AI, and

54
00:03:39,823 --> 00:03:45,088
makes sound decisions under uncertainty
will be more valuable than ever, with more

55
00:03:45,108 --> 00:03:48,033
leverage than any product manager before.

56
00:03:48,683 --> 00:03:51,424
The question isn't whether
AI will take the job.

57
00:03:51,444 --> 00:03:54,116
It's whether you'll grow into the new one.

58
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:01,596
So what does the super level look like?

59
00:04:01,846 --> 00:04:06,159
Think of it as a stack of seven
layers, each resting on the one below.

60
00:04:06,909 --> 00:04:09,134
Layer one:
customer obsession.

61
00:04:09,384 --> 00:04:11,074
Talk to users every week.

62
00:04:11,094 --> 00:04:15,810
Understand their goals, their
frustrations, and the workarounds they've

63
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:18,771
invented. AI can summarize
what people said.

64
00:04:18,791 --> 00:04:22,987
Only you can sit with them and
notice what they didn't say.

65
00:04:23,737 --> 00:04:26,190
Layer two: product
sense and taste.

66
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:30,124
Study great products and broken
ones, and write down why.

67
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:33,892
Taste is trained through
exposure, critique, and shipping.

68
00:04:33,912 --> 00:04:39,144
It's what lets you pick the right answer
from a hundred AI-generated options.

69
00:04:39,894 --> 00:04:42,161
Layer three:
AI fluency.

70
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:44,405
Understand how models work:

71
00:04:44,425 --> 00:04:49,296
context, hallucinations, retrieval,
fine-tuning, agents, and tools.

72
00:04:49,316 --> 00:04:54,332
Learn to write evaluations that
define what good behavior looks like.

73
00:04:54,352 --> 00:04:58,918
And know the cost and speed of
every AI feature you design.

74
00:04:59,668 --> 00:05:01,815
Layer four:
builder skills.

75
00:05:02,065 --> 00:05:05,403
Prototype your own ideas
with AI coding tools.

76
00:05:05,423 --> 00:05:11,333
Learn enough SQL and Python to answer your
own questions, and enough about APIs to

77
00:05:11,353 --> 00:05:12,905
know what's possible.

78
00:05:12,925 --> 00:05:16,834
The best product managers
of this era don't just tell.

79
00:05:16,854 --> 00:05:17,890
They show.

80
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,546
Layer five: data
and experimentation.

81
00:05:21,796 --> 00:05:25,791
Choose the metric that truly
matters. Design clean experiments.

82
00:05:25,811 --> 00:05:30,515
And read the results honestly,
especially when they disagree with you.

83
00:05:31,265 --> 00:05:33,317
Layer six:
domain depth.

84
00:05:33,567 --> 00:05:36,546
General AI knowledge is becoming common.

85
00:05:36,566 --> 00:05:41,740
Deep knowledge of an industry, like
healthcare, finance, education, or

86
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:46,692
climate, combined with AI, is
rare. Pick a domain, and go deep.

87
00:05:47,442 --> 00:05:50,464
Layer seven: leadership,
ethics, and trust.

88
00:05:50,714 --> 00:05:54,968
Align people who want different
things. Communicate with clarity.

89
00:05:54,988 --> 00:05:59,048
Catch risks to privacy, fairness,
and safety before they ship.

90
00:05:59,068 --> 00:06:03,581
As machines do more of the work,
the people we trust to decide become

91
00:06:03,601 --> 00:06:04,563
priceless.

92
00:06:05,513 --> 00:06:08,745
And on top sits the skill
that ties it all together:

93
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:11,407
judgment, sharpened by learning speed.

94
00:06:11,957 --> 00:06:14,304
Tools will change every few months.

95
00:06:14,324 --> 00:06:19,307
Your ability to learn, unlearn, and
decide well is the moat that lasts.

96
00:06:22,257 --> 00:06:25,107
Here is the knowledge to
build, in three rings.

97
00:06:25,557 --> 00:06:26,771
The product core:

98
00:06:26,791 --> 00:06:32,185
discovery, strategy, prioritization,
pricing and unit economics, go-to-market,

99
00:06:32,205 --> 00:06:35,470
and the psychology of
the people you serve.

100
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:36,938
The AI core:

101
00:06:36,958 --> 00:06:42,854
language models, retrieval, fine-tuning,
agents, evaluation, and the economics of

102
00:06:42,874 --> 00:06:44,928
running models at scale.

103
00:06:45,478 --> 00:06:47,613
And the responsibility core:

104
00:06:47,633 --> 00:06:53,779
privacy, security, fairness, safety, and
the laws taking shape around AI, like the

105
00:06:53,799 --> 00:06:55,907
European Union's AI Act.

106
00:07:01,057 --> 00:07:04,890
How do you actually get there?
Here is a path in four stages.

107
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:09,035
Stage one, the first
three months: foundations.

108
00:07:09,055 --> 00:07:13,803
Read the classics of product
management. Learn how AI models work.

109
00:07:13,823 --> 00:07:19,907
Use AI tools every day, and take apart
ten AI products to see how they were made.

110
00:07:20,657 --> 00:07:23,322
Stage two, months
three to six: build.

111
00:07:23,342 --> 00:07:27,796
Ship two or three small AI products
end to end, with real users.

112
00:07:27,816 --> 00:07:33,371
Write the spec, define the metrics, design
the evaluations, and publish what you

113
00:07:33,391 --> 00:07:34,261
learned.

114
00:07:35,011 --> 00:07:38,264
Stage three, months six
to twelve: specialize.

115
00:07:38,284 --> 00:07:39,656
Choose your domain.

116
00:07:39,676 --> 00:07:42,859
Learn its data, its
workflows, and its rules.

117
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:47,446
Get close to an AI feature at
work, or build one on your own.

118
00:07:48,196 --> 00:07:50,782
Stage four, years
one to three: lead.

119
00:07:50,802 --> 00:07:54,554
Own an AI product area.
Orchestrate people and agents.

120
00:07:54,574 --> 00:07:58,253
Mentor others, and share
your thinking in public.

121
00:08:01,203 --> 00:08:04,217
Along the way, seven
habits keep you resilient.

122
00:08:04,767 --> 00:08:06,446
Use AI every day.

123
00:08:06,646 --> 00:08:08,638
Talk to a customer every week.

124
00:08:08,838 --> 00:08:10,585
Ship something every month.

125
00:08:10,785 --> 00:08:12,420
Put your thinking in writing.

126
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:14,847
Study failures, including your own.

127
00:08:15,047 --> 00:08:16,394
Learn in public.

128
00:08:16,594 --> 00:08:20,047
And protect time to think
deeply, away from the screen.

129
00:08:22,597 --> 00:08:25,884
And if you're trying to break
in, there is more than one door.

130
00:08:26,434 --> 00:08:28,907
Associate product manager programs.

131
00:08:29,157 --> 00:08:33,885
An internal move from engineering,
design, data, support, or sales.

132
00:08:34,135 --> 00:08:36,499
A role at an early-stage startup.

133
00:08:36,849 --> 00:08:39,115
Or the most convincing path of all:

134
00:08:39,135 --> 00:08:43,067
build a real product yourself,
and let the results speak.

135
00:08:46,617 --> 00:08:48,736
The tools will keep getting smarter.

136
00:08:49,286 --> 00:08:51,719
Your job is to keep getting wiser.

137
00:08:52,769 --> 00:08:57,410
Because when machines can build almost
anything, the most valuable person in the

138
00:08:57,430 --> 00:09:00,500
room is the one who knows
what's worth building.
